# %%APP_NAME%%

A [Forge](https://github.com/fabriciobonjorno/forge-rust) application organized
around hexagonal architecture boundaries:

```text
src/domain          business rules; no I/O, HTTP, database or vendor types
src/application     commands, queries, use cases and ports
src/adapters        HTTP and other protocol translation
src/infrastructure  concrete database, cache, queue and telemetry mechanisms
src/bootstrap       composition root and process lifecycle
```

`tests/architecture.rs` fails when an inner ring imports an outer one.

## Development

%%if database%%
This application uses PostgreSQL. SQLx stays in the outer
infrastructure/bootstrap boundary; domain and application code do not depend on it.
The built-in Forge migrations create append-only audit storage first, then
principal/session/membership tables. Session bearer and CSRF values are persisted
only as 256-bit digests.

%%if docker%%
Start PostgreSQL with:

```bash
docker compose up -d db
```
%%end%%

Use the migration role only for schema changes, then the least-privilege runtime
role for the application:

```bash
export FORGE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL='%%DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL_LOCAL%%'
forge migrate                 # or: cargo run -- migrate

# Run once after migrations; the password is read from the terminal without echo.
cargo run -- bootstrap-account admin@example.com 'Acme Hospital'
unset FORGE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL

export FORGE_DATABASE_URL='%%DATABASE_RUNTIME_URL_LOCAL%%'
cargo run
```

Database-enabled applications include `infrastructure::audit::PgAuditSink`,
`infrastructure::auth::PostgresSessionStore`, and
`infrastructure::tenancy::PgMembershipStore`. The runtime role can append
structured security-audit events but cannot read, update, or delete audit
history.

The one-shot `bootstrap-account` command requires
`FORGE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL`, refuses to run after any principal exists, and
atomically creates the initial tenant, login, active `owner` membership and
`principal.bootstrap` / `tenant.bootstrap` audit evidence. The generated
application's deny-by-default RBAC policy must explicitly grant permissions to
`owner` before protected operations are allowed. Keep the migration credential
out of the serving process.

Create a reversible application migration with:

```bash
forge generate migration create_customers
```

This creates matching `.up.sql` and `.down.sql` files in `migrations/`.
Migration SQL is checked out with LF line endings so SQLx hashes remain stable
across operating systems.

%%end%%
```bash
cargo run                     # serves on http://127.0.0.1:3000
curl http://127.0.0.1:3000/health/live
forge check && forge lint && forge test
```

Binary commands: `serve` (default), `healthcheck`, `version`, `help`.
%%if database%%
Database-enabled binaries also support `migrate` and `rollback`.
`migrate` applies pending embedded migrations; `rollback` reverts the latest
reversible migration.
%%end%%

Configuration uses `FORGE_*` environment variables; unknown `FORGE_*` keys are
rejected at startup. Run `cargo run -- help` for the full list.
Forwarded client addresses are ignored by default. Behind a reverse proxy, set
`FORGE_TRUSTED_PROXIES` to the proxy's exact IP or narrow CIDR only when it
sanitizes/appends `X-Forwarded-For`; never trust a client-controlled forwarding
header by itself.

%%if docker%%
## Container

```bash
docker build -t %%APP_NAME%% .
%%if database%%
docker compose up --build     # PostgreSQL, one-shot migrations, then app
%%end%%
%%if !database%%
docker run --rm -p 3000:3000 %%APP_NAME%%
docker compose up --build
%%end%%
```

The image is built with `cargo build --release --locked`, so commit
`Cargo.lock`. The runtime stage is distroless and runs as uid 65532; the Docker
health check runs `%%APP_NAME%% healthcheck`.

%%if database%%
The Compose PostgreSQL credentials are development-only. It creates a dedicated
`app_migrator` role that owns schema changes and a separate `app_runtime` role
without schema ownership or `BYPASSRLS`. The database is stored in the named
`db-data` volume, and the app starts only after the database is healthy and
migrations complete successfully.

Tenant-sensitive persistence should open transactions through
`infrastructure::database::begin_tenant_transaction`. It installs the
authorized tenant/principal as transaction-local PostgreSQL settings; RLS policies
can read them with `current_setting('forge.tenant_id', true)` and
`current_setting('forge.principal_id', true)`.

`infrastructure::security` provides the concrete authentication mechanisms:
Argon2id password hashing (m=19456 KiB, t=2, p=1) on Tokio blocking workers,
independent 256-bit session/CSRF tokens from the OS CSPRNG, SHA-256 persistence
digests, a Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite=Lax `__Host-forge_session` cookie, generic
login denials, request session lookup and CSRF enforcement for unsafe methods.
Password verification yields an in-process proof bound to the observed credential
hash and version. Session issuance revalidates it under a PostgreSQL row lock and
commits any Argon2id policy upgrade, session digests, and success audit event in
one transaction before returning client secrets. Stale or disabled credentials
fail closed. PostgreSQL-backed authentication also atomically limits
attempts per normalized login and observed socket peer across replicas. Its
SHA-256 throttle keys are pseudonymous rather than anonymized, and forwarded
headers are ignored until a trusted-proxy policy is configured. The bearer
cookie and CSRF token are separate secrets;
never place the CSRF token
inside the HttpOnly session cookie.
When `FORGE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN` is configured, the generated app wires `GET
/auth/csrf` and `POST /auth/login`. The former returns a readable CSRF token
and sets its host-only cookie; send that token in `x-csrf-token` and the exact
configured `Origin` when posting `{"login":"...","password":"..."}`. The
login response sets the HttpOnly session cookie, rotates the CSRF cookie, and
returns the new CSRF token. Without the trusted origin, these routes are not
registered. `POST /auth/logout` requires the authenticated session cookie, its
session-bound `x-csrf-token`, and the exact configured `Origin`; it revokes the
session and appends `session.logout` in one transaction, then clears both
cookies. Authenticated route middleware, account provisioning, and tenant-aware
authorization are not wired yet; these endpoints are not a complete
account-management system.
